1. Yes.
Questions
2 (a) Canada is a member of the NATO alliance and has entered into agreement with the United States for the joint defence of the North American continent, and supports the peace preserving missions of the United Nations.
(b) The following are some of the more important measures to assist the population in surviving nuclear attack:
(i)	A warning system has been developed to warn the public of the likelihood of attack or fall-out;
(ii)	A continuing program of public information is operating in co-operation with provincial and local authorities to inform the public of the hazards of nuclear war and the measures they should take to protect themselves;
(iii)	An emergency broadcasting system is organized whereby the public, during an emergency, will be given information, advice and instructions;
(iv)	The Canadian army has prepared itself to carry out rescue operations in areas damaged or seriously contaminated;
(v)	Since 1962 radiation detection equipment has been made available to provinces so that radiation hazards can be detected and measured;
(vi)	Governments have been making plans and the required preparations to be able to do what will be needed of them at the time of emergency;
(vii)	The federal authorities have provided guidance and financial assistance to provinces by means of which many municipalities have developed plans whereby they can furnish essential services such as those with regard to fire, police and emergency welfare, even in the event of nuclear war;
(viii)	A war supplies agency is being organized to be responsible, among other matters, for mobilizing and equitably distributing stocks of food, fuel and clothing that survive attack;
(ix)	Many millions of dollars' worth of emergency health supplies have been stockpiled throughout Canada;
(x)	Since 1953, the federal government has maintained at Arnprior, Ontario, a civil defence college at which training has been provided for members of the military services and for federal, provincial and municipal employees and other persons nominated by the provinces and municipalities. This has been done wholly at federal cost.
3(a). The annual defence budget, as a rough estimate, although this includes some elements of support to (b) but does not include expenditures of other government departments related to defence.
